Start your free trial at pdq.com/ltt.

00:01:06.442 --> 00:01:09.025
(upbeat music)

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.073
- Right out of the gate,

00:01:16.073 --> 00:01:19.200
the 7900 Series is full of upgrades.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.620
It features AMD's brand
new RDNA 3 architecture,

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.500
up to 24 gigabytes of
much higher bandwidth,

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.530
GDDR6 memory, substantially
boosted core clocks

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.780
across the board, and this is great,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.990
the step down 7900 XT comes

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.500
with as many compute units
as AMD's previous flagship

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.070
with the beefier XTX rocking even more.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.637
Despite having more and
faster cores though,

00:01:43.637 --> 00:01:45.750
one of AMD's boldest claims is

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:49.920
that they've managed to
improve efficiency by 54%.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.210
This is thanks in large part

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.490
to AMD's high bandwidth chiplets design,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.980
the first of its kind in a modern GPU.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.770
What's more, they've beefed
up their ray accelerator cores

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.230
for real-time raytrace
lighting in supported games

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.130
and finally included AI
accelerator cores to bring Radeon

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.940
to feature parity with
NVIDIA and Intel's offerings.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.440
Unlike those offerings however,

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.870
the Radeon RX 7900 Series
cards we're looking

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.720
at today come equipped
with DisplayPort 2.1,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.720
which enables support for
up to 8K 165 hertz displays.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.830
Is that even necessary?

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.820
Who cares?

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.820
It's awesome and it's something
that NVIDIA's top end GPU,

00:02:28.887 --> 00:02:31.590
the RTX 4090 cannot do in spite

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.100
of its staggering $1,600 sticker price.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.460
All of this is while
actually fitting in your case

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.190
and costing $900 and a $1,000

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.860
for the XT and the XTX respectively.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.860
That is $200 to $300
less than the RTX 4080

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.320
that AMD says they should be
able to compete with for speed.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.690
Of course we won't be
taking their word for that.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.414
AMD has been known to be-

00:02:56.414 --> 00:02:57.247
(Linus coughing)

00:02:57.247 --> 00:02:58.560
optimistic.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.630
At 4K, it's clear out of the gate

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:01.680
and Shadow of the Tomb Raider

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.350
that whatever team red
fans might have hoped for,

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:09.350
the RX 7900 XTX is not AMD's
answer to the RTX 4090.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.390
It's got performance that
is realistically more

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.950
in line with the 4080,
though that is what AMD said.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.560
And this pattern continues in Hitman,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.430
Cyberpunk 2077, and Modern Warfare 2.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.990
Well, great, right?

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.510
I mean, why did I spend
all that time talking

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.390
about the importance of AMD's
ongoing driver developments?

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.860
Keep that in mind.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.990
Well, there's this for one thing.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.720
Forza Horizon 5 is a clear loss

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.300
for team red thanks to those subpar 1%

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.300
and 5% low frame rates, but
also this for another reason.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.663
That right there is a $1,000
GPU beating a $1,600 GPU

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.760
in F1 2022 and if the results I'm looking

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.130
at right now are any
indication of the kind

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.460
of raw power that AMD's
driver team has on tap here,

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.570
there is some serious aging
like fine wine potential

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.920
on the table.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.890
Of course, you should
never buy a promise though.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:12.890
And today AMD's best trails
NVIDIA's best by about 17%,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.650
which sounds bad until you realize

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.370
that NVIDIA's card costs 60% more.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.460
Compared to the 4080,
the XTX is a no-brainer

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.970
at 9% faster and 20% cheaper.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.060
And the 7900 XT does even better.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.290
I mean, sure.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:36.180
The 4080 is 7% faster but cost 33% more.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.600
1440p doesn't always show us
a difference compared to 4K.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.910
But here, we do see some strange behavior

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.790
in Forza that we retested several times.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.000
The 7000 Series is actually slower

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.510
than AMD's older 6000 Series cards.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.700
We think this could be
a problem with the game

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.280
as it's the only one out
of our suite that does it

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.800
and when we went to test
Forza's raytracing performance,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.430
we saw that it was in fact pretty broken.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.390
We also see the RTX 4080
pull ahead of even the XTX

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.150
in Shadow of the Tomb Raider
and Red Dead Redemption,

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.130
which is especially surprising considering

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.690
that virtually every other game

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.270
shows AMD closing the gap instead.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.370
The biggest example is in Modern Warfare 2

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.040
where the minimum frame rates
aren't quite up to the level

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.220
of the RTX 4090, but the
average frame rates are,

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.420
which again can set some
potential for optimization.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.550
In total, these numbers
put the 7900 Series

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.440
in a very good position to compete

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.870
with NVIDIA's 40 series to the point where

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.630
if traditional rasterized
gaming was all that existed,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:39.780
NVIDIA would need to lower their prices.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.730
But it isn't all that exists.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:46.380
And raytracing is, sadly,
still not AMD's strong suit.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.630
While substantially better in Cyberpunk

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.640
than the RX 6000 Series,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.460
these 7000 Series GPUs are behind NVIDIA

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.580
by at least a factor of 2 at 4K.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.770
At 1440p, AMD at least manages

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.930
to break the 30 FPS minimum mark,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.900
making the game playable
and you can use FSR

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:05.370
to render at a lower resolution

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.380
and upscale for smooth gameplay.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.250
But at that point,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.680
we could also do the same
thing for team green,

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.710
and the overall story
would still be the same.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:16.620
F1 2022 fares slightly
better for AMD at least,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.120
with the 7900 XTX pulling up
to within 16% of the RTX 4080

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.420
in minimum frame rates and
actually near parity on average,

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.510
which again gives me hope
that some of AMD's woes

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.730
with raytracing are a
matter of optimization

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.490
and that feeling only grows
with Shadow of the Tomb Raider

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.480
where even at 4K, it's within about 12%

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:39.180
of the 4090 and turning out nearly 60 FPS.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.060
It is an older game, but that's not bad.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.940
It's just that a good race ran,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.720
when the other guy ran
faster, is still an L.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.470
And ultimately, the RX
7900 XTX just couldn't even

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.290
come close to the RTX 4080's level

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.840
of performance with raytracing enabled.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.480
I suspect that most
people, myself included,

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.460
still don't use raytracing often,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.560
and won't really be affected.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.640
But given that these things

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.610
do still cost a thousand flipping dollars,

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.140
it's definitely a skid mark

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.650
on what has otherwise been a
pretty good showing so far.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:16.320
And I'm afraid there's
more where that came from.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:17.850
The professionals who have need

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.670
of this kind of graphical
horsepower will be, well,

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.860
disappointed isn't quite the right word,

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.000
but looking at these numbers,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.490
they're not gonna be excited either.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:29.550
Yes, Blender runs a good 8% to 20% faster

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.340
on the 7000 versus the 6000 Series,

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.850
but NVIDIA walks all
over AMD's entire stack

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.630
at between 3 to 4 times the performance.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.540
Thankfully, AMD's new encoding
engine helps them push ahead

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.820
of the RTX 4080 in DaVinci Resolve

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.910
in both H.264 and especially H.265,

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.130
which their encoding
engine seems to favor.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.860
That's a good 20% more
speed for the XTX there.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.000
Not bad, though the
4090 does remain on top.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.070
Procyon video and photo
are also pretty good

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.100
for AMD with performance
that I would call comparable.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.570
It's within a few percentage points

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:04.770
either way you look at it

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.710
and rounding out the video
editing suite is Topaz AI,

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.010
which despite the name,
is using GPU compute here,

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.650
rather than any AI cores.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:18.650
And wow, even the 7900 XT
outperforms the RTX 4090 here.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:23.340
This workload seems to heavily
favor AMD's Infinity Cache

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.300
as the 6000 Series cards
aren't too shabby either.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.360
SPECviewperf brings AMD
about as many wins as losses,

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.510
but the degree to which
the 7000 Series wins

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.480
over the competition is often much lower

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.460
than the losses that they suffer

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.050
with especially poor results

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:43.380
in 3DS Max, Maya, and SolidWorks,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.100
which even dip under the
performance of the 6000 Series.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:47.933
That's pretty rough.

00:08:47.933 --> 00:08:50.670
Those are kind of important applications.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.240
They pull off major wins in
CATIA, Creo, Energy, Medical,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.220
and especially Siemens NX,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.260
though as we've said in the past,

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.570
this is because the
version in SPECviewperf

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.240
doesn't support hardware
acceleration on NVIDIA,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.490
and actually this is
just gonna be excluded

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:08.190
from our averages when
we calculate it later on.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:09.780
SPECworkstation paints the Radeon

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.520
in a significantly better light though,

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.470
with product development, life sciences,

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.200
and energy all pushing
well past team green,

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:17.700
but media and entertainment,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.090
as well as GPU compute
still stay with NVIDIA,

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:25.350
likely thanks to openCL's
relative obscurity next to Cuda.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.900
Despite the blows the 7900
Series cards took though,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.250
their beefed up media engines

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.200
and GPU compute horsepower placed them

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.050
within an average of 2%
to 10% of team green.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.730
Given the wide performance spread we saw,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:36.960
depending on the workload,

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.000
potential perspective
professional people will want

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.200
to think long and hard

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.930
about which capabilities
are most important

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.570
to them before making a final decision.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:46.470
Changing gears a little,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.670
we found something interesting

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.440
when we were testing Resizable BAR

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.200
on both Intel and AMD platforms.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.450
It seems like the
performance improvements,

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.310
regardless of whether you have a team blue

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.550
or team red CPU, can be
expected to be pretty similar

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.400
despite AMD heavily
marketing Smart Access Memory

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.720
as a selling point for the
Ryzen plus Radeon ecosystem.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.840
Bear in mind that this is a
sample size of just two games,

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.030
but without Resizable BAR,

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:13.650
we're figuring you can expect anywhere

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.830
from a 10% to 35% reduction in performance

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:20.220
regardless of which side you swing for.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.200
As for AMD's updated video encoder,

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.060
there's a lot to be excited about here.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.520
AV1 support and baked in machine learning

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.860
to improve quality at the top of my list.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.780
But despite receiving our cards early,

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.280
we actually only had a few days

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:33.330
to test these things prior

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.640
to embargo because we
didn't have a driver,

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.190
so we were forced to push
our image quality comparisons

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.023
to a separate video

00:10:39.023 --> 00:10:41.730
that you can expect to see
in the next couple of weeks.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:43.920
One of AMD's other selling points is

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.580
its smaller footprint compared
to NVIDIA's 40 Series GPUs.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.170
And I was concerned that this would come

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:50.400
at the expense of thermals,

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.010
so I was extremely pleased to see

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:56.190
that AMD's new cooler
design has the 7000 Series

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.670
actually performing better
than the 6000 Series.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.310
With that said though, AMD
does not have the luxury

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.110
of only competing with themselves

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.170
and NVIDIA is clearly in
the better position here.

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:08.670
Though with that said,

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.550
both of the NVIDIA cards we
tested have substantially

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.400
larger heat sinks with fan off modes,

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.430
while AMD's new cards
always ran their fans.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:20.164
It's probable that designs
from the like of Asus

00:11:20.164 --> 00:11:24.303
or PowerColor will run much
cooler and hopefully quieter.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:27.630
Now, we need to talk about core clocks

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.670
because they're a little
different this generation

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:31.650
thanks to the decoupling of the front end

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:33.150
and the shader clocks.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:34.200
The idea here is

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.260
that the front end will benefit
more from higher clocks,

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:39.720
while the more numerous
shader units crunch data

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.550
in parallel at a lower clock speed,

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.400
resulting in a better balance
of performance and efficiency.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:45.900
And you can see that in action here

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:48.266
where both the 7900 XTX

00:11:48.266 --> 00:11:51.900
and XT have a consistent 200 megahertz gap

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.370
between the two clocks.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:56.130
And even in a power
virus, like MSI Kombustor,

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.420
these clocks remained higher

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.220
than the previous generation.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.580
Clock stability in-game is
similar to the 6000 Series cards,

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:05.400
but NVIDIA's 40 Series
manages flatter lines

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:06.233
throughout the run,

00:12:06.233 --> 00:12:08.880
suggesting that AMD is trying
to be pretty aggressive

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.980
with managing power consumption,

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:13.650
which makes sense because AMD talked a lot

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.660
about performance per
watt in their marketing.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.700
And looking at these in-game power graphs?

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:18.720
No?

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.100
Not seems to suggest

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:23.700
that their self congratulations
are well justified.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:26.940
The Radeon RX 7900 XTX
drew comparable power

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.520
to the 4080 and this
held even when we hit it

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.960
with MSI Kombustor.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:32.970
The XT was even better still,

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:35.160
staying in line with its predecessor.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.890
But what's interesting is when
we take a magnifying glass

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:41.220
to how many watts the 7000
Series pulls per frame.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.910
In this example, while the
raw wattage drops much closer

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:48.870
to the RTX 4080, when we cap
the frame rate to a hundred,

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:52.350
the total watts per frame
increases substantially.

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:53.430
This suggests that

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:57.210
while the RTX 4080 is able to
throttle itself effectively,

00:12:57.210 --> 00:13:00.630
AMD is actually having some
trouble with power management.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:03.330
And AMD's idle power
consumption numbers seem

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.890
to corroborate this.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:09.330
Where the RTX 4080 idles
at around 15 to 20 watts,

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:13.170
the 7900 XTX regularly sucks back 40

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:17.880
and sometimes over 50 watts,
while doing nothing at all.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:20.010
What's even more baffling
is that we noticed

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.860
what seems like a major
bug during our testing.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.860
Depending on the monitor
that we plugged into the GPU,

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:29.640
we saw wildly different total
system power at the wall.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.000
From a respectable 52 watts
with nothing plugged in

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.180
to a whopping 171 watts

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:39.420
with our 27 inch ROG Swift bench display.

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:43.650
Using some other monitors even
caused performance to drop.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:46.470
Now, given that I have a high-end display

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.030
and I live in Vancouver, Canada

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:51.330
where energy is the one
thing that is still cheap,

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:52.920
none of this is a deal breaker for me.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:56.310
But if I was in Europe where
energy prices have surged,

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.130
pun intended, it would give me pause

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:02.010
before I was willing to look at team red.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:03.450
Adding insult to injury,

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:06.510
this high idle power draw means
that the fans are spinning

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:09.420
at all times, kicking that
heat out into your room

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:11.610
even if you're just scrolling Reddit,

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:13.230
meaning that the 7900 Series

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:16.140
is gonna be a little less
comfortable to live with.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.840
Maybe if you go team red,
you could pick up a blank T,

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.960
rather than a retro polar
fleece from lttstore.com.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:22.920
Space heater.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:24.150
One possible explanation

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:26.250
for this high idle power
consumption could be

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:30.030
that this is a similar situation
to the Verizon 7 5800X3D.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:33.990
That CPU's use of 3D V-cache
limited its stability

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:37.110
if voltages weren't dialed in just right

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.600
and we could be witnessing
similar teething issues

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.820
with this new chiplet architecture

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.650
and supporting that theory is the fact

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:46.620
that the driver straight up
locks out power adjustments

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:49.140
below 90% of total board power.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:50.550
When we asked them about this,

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.470
AMD played pretty coy with us,

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.480
which tells us either we're right

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.030
or that they're not sure either.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:58.050
Now it's time to talk about

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.270
what this data means for the GPU market.

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.070
First of all, anyone who was hoping

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.990
that the 7900 Series would come in

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.220
and compete head-to-head

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:09.240
against the RTX 4090 will
be sorely disappointed,

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:10.560
at least for today.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:12.420
We saw brief glimpses of glory,

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:15.030
but the RTX 4090 remains on its throne,

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:16.980
making it tough to dislodge its price

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:20.160
from that TI Titan perch

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:23.040
that it currently occupies
as a Halo product.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.980
But while AMD doesn't have
a direct competitor to it,

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:30.510
I still think the 7900 XTX
is a much better value.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:32.940
As for the RTX 4080, well,

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.460
the comparison gets a
little more complicated.

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.800
Even the XT model comes really close

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.140
in traditional rendering
and the value proposition

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:43.980
of either 7900 GPU is a damning indictment

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.240
of NVIDIA's pricing.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:48.120
But AMD does remain on the back foot

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:49.650
in raytracing performance

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.570
and that's something
that NVIDIA can point to

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.580
and claim makes up the difference,

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.010
even if nobody seriously cares.

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:58.290
It'll take people like me running out

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.920
and buying 7900 Series
cards instead of 40 Series

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.620
to make Nvidia realize
that they're out to lunch.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:08.010
But wait, why are we saying
this now, you might think.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:10.320
We took a lot of flack for covering

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:13.470
for NVIDIA's pricing in the
RTX 4080 review, didn't we?

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:15.300
Well, not so much.

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:17.820
First off, we compared it
to cards that were available

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:19.980
at the time it released
and we ended the video

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:22.890
with a message to wait for RDNA 3.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:26.910
And we are reviewing the 7900
Series in much the same way.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.130
Second, it had already been made known

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:32.730
that the RX 7900 XTX
was not going to compete

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.550
with the 4090, which depending
on how you look at it,

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:40.470
means that either the RTX 4080
is actually a 90 class card

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:41.550
and priced accordingly,

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:44.730
with the 4090 being a TI or something,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:49.110
or the 7900 XT and XTX are
the current gen equivalent

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:54.110
of the 6800 and 6800 XT and
are themselves overpriced.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:56.640
Basically, it's kind of a
game of pick your villain

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.410
at this point, cuz the reality is

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:02.400
that $600 top of the line
GPUs are a thing of the past

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:05.040
and that was an assumption
that our review made

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.230
but did not fully explain.

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:08.670
It's worth pointing out, by the way,

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:10.380
since I'm being a downer right now anyway,

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:13.860
that while AMD supports DisplayPort 2.1

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:15.990
and NVIDIA's 40 Series does not,

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:17.640
AMD went with a half measure

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:21.600
by supporting only the UHBR 13.5 spec.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.610
What this means is that
it can only achieve

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:25.860
its lofty resolution
and frame rate support

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:27.900
through display stream compression.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.270
DSC allows up to three
times more bandwidth

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:34.200
that is both near zero
latency and visually lossless,

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.980
though that doesn't mean that
there is no quality loss.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:39.810
Practically speaking, this
won't be a problem even

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:40.980
if it were noticeable given

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:42.780
that these GPUS aren't really capable

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:45.330
of pushing those resolutions
and frame rates anyway,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.490
but we brought it up
with the RTX 40 Series,

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.040
so we're bringing it up here
for the sake of completeness.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.830
It means that those new
8K ultra-wide displays

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:55.410
being shown up at CES
will just barely be able

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:58.020
to run at 85 Hertz without DSC.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.130
But I would never expect

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.350
that to be a problem for an actual user.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:04.020
One slightly problematic thing
that I do need to address is

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:07.650
that we've taken a very glass
half full approach today

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:08.880
and I can understand why some

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:10.650
of you might be frustrated by it.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:11.483
We've gone

00:18:11.483 --> 00:18:15.390
and spun AMD's potential
future driver improvements

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:19.320
as a good thing when
actually they're kind of bad.

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:21.090
Another perspective is that it means

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:23.400
that AMD is releasing these products even

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:26.160
though they haven't really
finished optimizing them.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.770
And if you've kind of felt
that way up to this point,

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.540
I totally get where you're coming from.

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.270
We ran into some kind of bizarre,

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:34.842
no really bizarre crashes,

00:18:34.842 --> 00:18:36.840
and there are certainly features

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.850
that could be added or refined.

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:42.600
But in fairness to our
charitable approach today,

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.700
AMD is pricing these GPUs according

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:47.520
to what they are now
and their track record

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:50.190
for fixing these things
is actually pretty good

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:51.720
even if sometimes it takes a while

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.227
as they focus on new features like FSR 3.0

00:18:55.338 --> 00:18:58.230
and the Hyper-RX performance optimizer

00:18:58.230 --> 00:18:59.970
that have yet to launch.

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:02.130
And if this is where performance is now,

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:06.510
with some of what broken,
certainly unoptimized drivers,

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:09.930
AMD clearly has some great
hardware on their hands here.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.150
They even brought back the USB-C port,

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.280
something that is great
for hooking up a monitor

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:16.980
via a single cable and a godsend

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.540
for enthusiasts like myself.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:21.660
I'm still mad that NVIDIA
removed it after the 20 Series.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:25.710
so the RX 7900 XTX is
going into my gaming rig

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:27.690
and I'm officially switching to team red

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:32.690
for the first time since the 3870.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:33.713
- [Anthony] Wow.

00:19:33.713 --> 00:19:35.370
- Goodness gracious.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:37.080
The performance is good enough.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:38.610
The price is good enough.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:40.620
Well, at least compared
to the competition.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.660
And the downsides are small enough

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.390
that I am comfortable making the leap.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:46.800
Just like I'm gonna comfortably leap

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:49.047
to this segue from our sponsor.

